# ============ B1：7-9 句真题长度语篇（95-115 词，中考真题规格） ============
# 话题族：①动画卡通 ②校园规则 ③环境保护 ④志愿服务 ⑤传统节日 ⑥健康运动 ⑦科技网络 ⑧旅行城市
#         ⑨名人故事 ⑩阅读学习 ⑪动物保护 ⑫家庭亲情 ⑬友谊相处 ⑭天气自然 ⑮安全自护 ⑯饮食习惯
#         ⑰音乐艺术 ⑱考试心态 ⑲梦想职业 ⑳社区邻里 ㉑发明科学 ㉒诚实品格 ㉓零花钱消费 ㉔户外露营 ㉕野生动物
B1_TEXTS = [
# —— ① 动画卡通 ——
"Mickey Mouse is one of the most famous cartoon characters in the world. Walt Disney created him in 1928, and he first appeared in a short black-and-white film. Mickey is a cheerful mouse who always tries to help his friends. Although he often gets into trouble, he never gives up. Millions of children have grown up watching his adventures. Today, Mickey is more than ninety years old, but he still makes people smile. Disney said that Mickey's magic comes from his simple and kind heart. His picture can be seen on clothes, bags and toys all over the world.",
"Peppa Pig is a British cartoon character loved by children everywhere. She is a little pig who lives with her family in a red house on a hill. Every episode tells a simple story about family life. Peppa loves jumping in muddy puddles with her younger brother George. Her stories teach children about sharing, helping and saying sorry. The programme has been translated into many languages. Parents like it because the stories are short and gentle. Children like it because Peppa sounds like a real friend. In every story, something small goes wrong, but the family solves it together.",
"Doraemon is a blue robot cat from the future. He came back in time to help a boy called Nobita with his homework and problems. Doraemon has a magic pocket that holds amazing tools. Whenever Nobita is in trouble, Doraemon takes out a useful machine. Sometimes the machines cause even bigger problems. In the end, Nobita learns that hard work matters more than magic. The cartoon has been popular in China for more than thirty years. Many students say Doraemon feels like a helpful friend from childhood. Young viewers enjoy the funny tools and the warm friendship at the same time.",
"Ne Zha is a hero from Chinese traditional stories. The 2019 film Ne Zha attracted millions of viewers in cinemas. In the film, Ne Zha is born with magic power that people fear. Everyone thinks he will become an evil spirit, but he chooses to fight for his town. His famous line 'my fate is decided by myself, not by heaven' moved many young people. The film mixes exciting action with a story about family love. It shows that courage and choice matter more than one's background. After seeing it, many students began to read the original novel. Its success also started a new wave of interest in traditional culture.",
# —— ② 校园生活与规则 ——
"Our school has a long history of more than eighty years. It stands at the foot of a green hill in the centre of the city. The old buildings are painted light yellow and full of trees. Every morning, students read English aloud under the big banyan tree. Although the school is old, its labs and library are modern. Teachers here are strict but always ready to help. Last year, our school won the city basketball championship. Students feel proud to study in such a warm place. Many graduates return every spring to visit their old classrooms.",
"School rules sometimes feel boring, but they protect everyone. For example, students must not run in the corridors because someone may fall down. We must wear school uniforms so that no one feels different because of clothes. Mobile phones are not allowed in class, since they take attention away from lessons. If everyone follows the rules, the school day runs smoothly. Students who break rules are asked to explain and put things right. In this way, rules become a lesson about respect. Our class made its own rules together last month. Surprisingly, nobody broke them after that.",
"A school day in China usually starts before eight o'clock. Students arrive early and hand in their homework at the door. The morning reading period lasts twenty minutes and fills the halls with sound. Between classes, there is a ten-minute break for water and fresh air. Lunch is served in the canteen, where students choose from several dishes. In the afternoon, there are fewer lessons and more activities. Some students join clubs, while others play basketball on the playground. The bell rings at about half past five, and buses line up outside. For many students, the bus ride home is the best time to relax.",
"Homework is a hot topic in almost every family. Some students finish it quickly, but others stay up late. Experts say that short breaks between subjects improve concentration. If a student feels stuck, the best step is to move to an easier task. Copying answers may save ten minutes but destroys real learning. Good homework habits begin with a clean desk and a quiet room. Parents can help by asking questions instead of giving answers. In the end, homework belongs to the student. The purpose is not the grade, but the growth that comes from thinking.",
# —— ③ 环境保护 ——
"Our planet is facing serious environmental problems. Every year, millions of tons of plastic enter the oceans and harm sea animals. Factories and cars send smoke into the air, which makes the climate warmer. If we do nothing now, future generations will pay a heavier price. The good news is that everyone can do something small. Students can refuse plastic bags and take cloth bags when shopping. Schools can start recycling programmes for paper and bottles. When small actions add up, they become real change. Protecting the earth is not a choice; it is our duty.",
"Sorting rubbish seems troublesome, but it makes a real difference. In many cities, bins are now painted in four colours for different kinds of waste. Used paper and plastic bottles belong in the recycling bins. Kitchen waste can be turned into fertilizer for plants. Dangerous waste, such as old batteries, needs special treatment. If rubbish is not sorted, everything is buried or burned, and the soil suffers. Our class started a sorting competition last term. Within a month, we reduced our classroom waste by half. Each small bin is a small promise to the future.",
"Water is precious, yet many people waste it every day. A running tap can waste several litres of water in one minute. In some parts of the world, children walk kilometres to fetch clean water. We can save water in simple ways at home and at school. Turning off the tap while brushing teeth saves a bowl of water a day. Reusing laundry water for flushing toilets helps even more. If every family saves a little, a city saves millions of litres. Saving water is saving life itself. Let us act before the last drop becomes our tears.",
"Trees are the lungs of our city, but they are disappearing fast. Old trees are cut down to make space for new buildings and roads. Without trees, streets become hotter and noisier in summer. Trees also clean the air and provide homes for birds. Last year, our community planted two hundred young trees along the river. Students watered them every week during the dry season. Now the riverside path has become the coolest walk in town. A city with trees is a city with a future. When you plant a tree, you plant hope.",
# —— ④ 志愿服务 ——
"Volunteering gives students a chance to see the world outside textbooks. Last month, our class visited a nursing home near our school. We cleaned the rooms, washed clothes and told stories to the elderly. Some students sang old songs that reminded the elderly of their youth. Others simply sat and listened, because listening is also a kind of care. One old man showed us photos of his school in the 1960s. We realised that respect for the elderly is a bridge between generations. Before leaving, we gave them cards we had made by hand. The visit taught us more than any lesson about kindness.",
"Charity begins at home, but it should not stop there. Every winter, our school collects warm clothes for children in mountain villages. Students bring clean coats, scarves and books they no longer need. Teachers check everything and pack the boxes carefully. A truck carries the boxes to the villages before the coldest days arrive. Last year, the children there wrote back with thank-you letters. Their simple words made our whole school cry and smile. Helping others does not require much money, only a willing heart. A small box can carry a big warmth across the mountains.",
"Blood donation saves lives, but many people still know little about it. Doctors say a healthy adult can give blood several times a year. The blood is separated into parts that help patients after operations. Donors only feel a small pinch and need a short rest. Some students worry that donation makes the body weak, which is not true. Our school invited a doctor to explain the process last week. After the talk, several teachers signed up to give blood. A single bag of blood may keep a family whole. Being a donor means being a silent hero.",
"Community service teaches responsibility that classrooms cannot. Every Saturday, some of our students work in the neighbourhood library. They arrange books, help children find stories and fix broken chairs. At first, they thought the work was too simple for them. Later they discovered that small tasks keep a shared place alive. A tidy library invites more readers, especially on hot afternoons. One librarian said our students remind adults of their duty. Service is not about praise; it is about showing up. When everyone contributes, the community becomes a warmer home.",
# —— ⑤ 传统节日 ——
"The Spring Festival is the most important festival in China. Families clean their houses and paste red paper-cuts on the windows. On New Year's Eve, the whole family gathers for a huge dinner. Dumplings and fish are traditional dishes that symbolise good luck. Children receive red envelopes with money from their elders. At midnight, fireworks light up the sky and drive away bad luck. During the holiday, people visit relatives and exchange warm greetings. Although traditions are changing, the feeling of reunion never changes. For Chinese people, going home is the true meaning of the festival.",
"The Mid-Autumn Festival falls on the fifteenth day of the eighth lunar month. On that night, the moon is said to be the roundest and brightest of the year. Families sit together in courtyards or on balconies to admire the moon. Mooncakes with lotus paste and egg yolk are the special food of the day. Children carry lanterns of different shapes through the streets. The festival also remembers the poet Li Bai and his love of the moon. In many cities, families now order mooncakes online instead of baking them. However, sitting together under the same moon remains the centre of the night. Distance cannot separate people who share one moon.",
"Dragon Boat Festival honours an ancient poet called Qu Yuan. More than two thousand years ago, Qu Yuan served his country with loyalty. When his state fell, he jumped into the Miluo River in despair. Local people rowed boats and threw rice into the water to save him. That is why we race dragon boats and eat zongzi today. The boat drums beat fast, and the rowers pull together like one body. The festival was added to the world heritage list in 2009. Students now learn the story in Chinese lessons. The spirit of love for one's country still beats in the races.",
"Christmas is a warm festival in many Western countries. Families decorate a tree with lights, stars and small gifts weeks before the day. On Christmas Eve, children hang stockings by the bed and wait for Santa Claus. In the morning, everyone opens presents and shares a special breakfast. Streets and shops are full of music and colour in December. In China, more and more young people celebrate it as a fun occasion. Friends exchange apples and small gifts instead of big dinners. Whatever the tradition, the festival carries one message: share joy with the people you love. Warm hearts make any day a festival.",
# —— ⑥ 健康与运动 ——
"Exercise does more than build strong muscles. Scientists say that thirty minutes of daily movement improves sleep and memory. Students who run or play ball games often concentrate better in class. Exercise also helps the brain produce chemicals that make us feel happy. However, many teenagers spend their free time sitting with phones. Doctors warn that too little movement leads to weak eyes and heavy bodies. A small change, such as walking to school, can start a healthier life. Choose a sport you enjoy, and you will keep doing it. A strong body carries a strong mind.",
"The school basketball team trained hard for the city final. They practised shooting every morning before lessons began. Their coach said that teamwork matters more than personal stars. In the final, the score stayed close until the last two minutes. Then Tom made a long shot from the corner and the crowd went wild. The team won by three points and hugged each other on the court. After the match, they thanked the coach with one voice. Sport teaches students how to win and how to lose. The real prize is the friendship built during training.",
"Sleep is the cheapest medicine, but teenagers often ignore it. Studies show that students need about nine hours of sleep each night. Staying up late to play games damages both health and grades. A tired brain learns slowly and forgets quickly. Good sleep habits begin with a fixed bedtime and no phones in bed. Drinking milk and avoiding coffee at night also help. If students sleep well, their mood becomes steadier as well. Schools now start later in some cities for this reason. A good night is the best preparation for a bright day.",
"The marathon in our city attracts runners of all ages. Last Sunday, more than ten thousand people took part in the race. The course passed riverside roads, old bridges and green parks. Some runners wore colourful costumes to raise money for charity. A seventy-year-old grandpa finished the whole distance on foot. Spectators clapped and handed cups of water to tired runners. For runners, the hardest moment is often the thirtieth kilometre. Those who keep going discover strength they never knew. One step at a time is how every long road is walked.",
# —— ⑦ 科技与网络 ——
"Smart phones have changed the way students learn. Lessons, dictionaries and libraries now fit into a small screen. Students can watch experiment videos and listen to English songs anywhere. However, the same phone also brings games and short videos that eat time. Experts suggest turning off notifications during study hours. A phone is a tool, and the user decides whether it helps or harms. Some schools ask students to hand in phones at the classroom door. This rule feels strict, but it protects concentration. A wise student treats the screen as a window, not a wall.",
"Online learning became normal for students all over the world. During special periods, classrooms moved into small screens at home. Students watched lessons in bed clothes and paused teachers freely. At first, everyone enjoyed the freedom and the extra sleep. Soon, however, many found it hard to focus without classmates nearby. Teachers learned to use quizzes and chat rooms to keep students active. The experience proved that technology cannot replace human teachers. It can only carry their voice across distance. Screen or no screen, curiosity remains the real engine.",
"Robots are entering kitchens, factories and even classrooms. A robot can repeat the same action a thousand times without tiredness. In some hospitals, small robots deliver medicine along the corridors. However, robots still cannot understand human feelings. They cannot comfort a crying child or encourage a nervous student. That is why teachers, nurses and parents remain irreplaceable. In the future, dull and dangerous work will belong to machines. Creative and caring work will belong to people. The heart of a lesson will always be human.",
"The internet stores a library larger than any building. With a few clicks, students can read news from other countries. They can watch lectures by famous professors for free. However, not everything online is true or kind. False news spreads faster than facts because it sounds surprising. Students must check information from at least two reliable sources. They should also protect personal information carefully online. Search engines answer questions, but they never ask them. A good digital citizen doubts first and shares later. Click slowly, and every page becomes a classroom.",
# —— ⑧ 旅行与城市 ——
"Travelling opens a window that no classroom can provide. When students visit a new city, they meet different food, dialects and customs. A trip to Xi'an makes history lessons about the Tang Dynasty feel real. Walking along the West Lake helps students understand Chinese landscape paintings. Travelling also teaches practical skills like reading maps and asking for directions. On the road, small problems appear without warning and demand quick thinking. Families who travel together create memories that last for decades. The best souvenir is not a model but a story. The world is a book, and travel opens its pages.",
"Beijing welcomes visitors with both ancient wonders and modern life. The Great Wall winds over mountains like a stone dragon. The Forbidden City shows the life of emperors from six hundred years ago. At the same time, the city grows modern parks, subways and art centres. Visitors can taste roast duck in a small hutong restaurant. They can also watch the latest plays in brand-new theatres. Old and new stand side by side without any conflict. That mixture is exactly the charm of the capital. Every hutong tells a quiet story of ordinary lives.",
"Travelling by train is a slow pleasure in a fast age. High-speed trains now connect most big cities in a few hours. On board, passengers read, sleep or watch fields slide past the window. Unlike planes, trains bring travellers into the centre of a city. Families with children often prefer them because there is space to walk. Train travel also produces much less pollution than flying. Students can reach mountain villages and small towns that planes skip. Looking out of the window, one sees the real shape of a country. Sometimes the journey matters more than the destination.",
"Tourism brings money, but it can also hurt fragile places. Every year, millions of visitors climb the Great Wall and leave litter behind. Some ancient walls are worn down by thousands of touching hands. Beaches suffer when tourists leave plastic in the sand. Local people sometimes lose their quiet homes to noisy hotels. That is why many places now limit the number of daily visitors. Students can help by taking rubbish away and keeping voices low. Respect is the best ticket any traveller can carry. Protect the places you love, and they will welcome the next guest.",
# —— ⑨ 名人故事 ——
"Yuan Longping devoted his life to fighting hunger. As a young scientist, he chose rice research when few thought it important. He worked in the fields under the hot sun for decades. In the 1970s, his hybrid rice raised harvests by twenty per cent. This discovery helped feed not only China but also many other countries. Students know him as 'the father of hybrid rice'. He remained modest and said he was simply a farmer who loved rice. His work shows that science serves ordinary people best. A single idea can fill billions of bowls.",
"Tu Youyou changed medicine with a plant from ancient books. She read traditional Chinese texts and found a recipe for treating fevers. After hundreds of failed experiments, she finally extracted artemisinin. The medicine has saved millions of lives in the fight against malaria. In 2015, she received the Nobel Prize for her discovery. Her story proves that old wisdom can answer modern questions. She worked quietly in a Beijing laboratory for most of her career. Young scientists can learn patience from her example. One book, one plant, one determined mind.",
"J.K. Rowling wrote her first Harry Potter book in a small Edinburgh cafe. At that time, she was a single mother with little money. She wrote in the mornings while her baby slept beside her. Twelve publishers rejected the first book before one said yes. Today, the series has sold more than five hundred million copies. Her story encourages anyone who faces repeated failure. Rowling often says that rock bottom became the solid base of her life. Imagination turned her difficult days into golden ones. Never underestimate a person with a story to tell.",
"Lang Ping is a legend in Chinese volleyball. She played as a young star in the 1980s and won world fame. Later she became a coach and led the Chinese women's team to gold. Her players say she is strict in training and warm outside the court. When the team loses, she takes responsibility first. When they win, she pushes them to forget the score. Her life shows that sport builds character as much as trophies. Young athletes can learn her calm under pressure. Every spike begins with a thousand silent practices.",
# —— ⑩ 阅读与学习 ——
"Reading is to the mind what exercise is to the body. Books carry students to places they have never seen. Through reading, they can talk with scientists, poets and explorers. Students who read widely usually write with more ease. Their vocabulary grows quietly, page after page. However, many teenagers say they have no time to read. In fact, fifteen minutes before sleep is enough to finish a book a month. Choosing books slightly above your level brings the fastest growth. A quiet lamp and a good book are faithful friends.",
"Good study habits beat long hours of painful effort. Students should study in focused blocks of about forty minutes. Short breaks between blocks keep the brain fresh. Reviewing notes within a day helps memory stay for years. Mistakes are the best teachers if they are collected in a special notebook. Studying in the same quiet place trains the brain to concentrate quickly. Explaining a lesson to a friend tests real understanding. Hard work without method is like running in the wrong direction. The wise student works smarter, not merely harder.",
"Our school library holds more than fifty thousand books. It opens early and stays busy until evening. Students borrow storybooks, science magazines and English novels. The librarian knows every student's favourite shelf. Reading week last month brought a small reading storm. Classes competed to record the most pages read. Some students read eight books in three weeks. In the quiet corners, shy readers grow into brave speakers. The head teacher says reading builds patience in a fast world. Turning pages is the quietest journey a student can take.",
"Note-taking looks simple, but good notes change grades. Copying every word from the blackboard wastes time and energy. Smart students write key words, draw small maps and mark questions. Coloured pens help separate main ideas from small details. After class, notes should be reviewed within the same day. Some students rewrite messy notes into neat ones before sleep. A clean notebook is a mirror of a clear mind. Teachers can tell a student's thinking from the margins of a notebook. The pen remembers what the mind may forget.",
# —— ⑪ 动物与宠物 ——
"Pandas are national treasures, but they once faced real danger. Fifty years ago, there were fewer than two thousand wild pandas. Hunters killed them for fur, and forests disappeared year by year. The government built nature reserves and strict laws followed. Scientists studied panda habits and helped sick animals recover. Bamboo planting projects gave pandas more food and more space. Today, the number of wild pandas has grown to about eighteen hundred. In 2016, pandas were moved from 'endangered' to 'vulnerable'. A gentle animal taught a nation the value of patience.",
"Stray animals need human care more than anything else. In cities, homeless cats and dogs search rubbish bins for food. Winters are especially hard for animals without warm shelters. Students can help by feeding them and calling rescue groups. Some schools keep a small box of pet food for street animals. Volunteers take injured animals to friendly vets for treatment. Adopting instead of buying reduces the number of strays. Animals cannot speak, but their eyes carry a clear request. Every small bowl of food is a small act of love.",
"Elephants are the largest animals walking on land. An adult elephant eats more than one hundred kilos of plants a day. They live in family groups led by the oldest female. Elephants greet friends by touching trunks and flapping ears. Sadly, hunters kill them for ivory tusks that sell at high prices. Without protection, wild elephants may disappear within decades. Many countries now ban the trade of ivory completely. Tourists can help by refusing anything made of ivory. Never buy ivory, and no elephant will die for an ornament.",
"Birds migrate thousands of kilometres every autumn and spring. They fly in V-shaped groups to save energy during long journeys. Wetlands along the coast are their most important rest stations. However, many wetlands have been turned into farms and buildings. Some birds cannot find food and die before reaching home. Students in coastal cities now count birds and record their numbers. They write reports and send them to bird protection centres. Their lists help scientists protect the stopping places. Watching a sky full of wings is a gift worth guarding.",
# —— ⑫ 家庭与亲情 ——
"Family is the first school every child attends. Parents teach language, table manners and simple values long before school. A warm family gives children courage to face the outside world. However, modern life leaves parents little time at home. Many fathers and mothers work late and check phones at dinner. Experts suggest at least twenty minutes of real talk every day. Listening without judging is the greatest gift a parent can offer. Children who feel heard at home speak up in class. The best inheritance is not money but attention.",
"Grandparents hold a special place in Chinese families. They look after children while parents work long hours. In return, children bring them news from school and the internet. Many grandparents live far away and use video calls every week. My grandmother tells me stories about her village in the 1960s. She says life was hard, but neighbours were close like family. Their generation teaches us gratitude for ordinary days. During festivals, three generations cook and eat around one table. Age brings wrinkles, but it also brings warmth.",
"Sibling rivalry is normal, but it can teach cooperation. Brothers and sisters share rooms, toys and their parents' attention. Small quarrels about the remote control happen in every home. Psychologists say such fights help children learn to negotiate. Parents should treat children fairly instead of comparing them. My elder sister once broke my model plane in a moment of anger. She apologised the next day and helped me repair it. Now we choose films together and take turns. A sibling is the first friend a child ever has.",
"The generation gap worries many families. Parents grew up without the internet and value stability. Children grow up with screens and value freedom and speed. Each side sometimes thinks the other simply does not understand. The gap narrows when families learn each other's world. A father who tries his son's game gains new respect. A daughter who hears her mother's youth stories understands her rules. Communication works better than argument in every home. Differences become interesting when both sides stay curious. Listening turns a gap into a bridge.",
# —— ⑬ 友谊与相处 ——
"True friendship is rare and worth protecting. A real friend notices when you fall silent and asks why. Friends share joy, but they also share difficult homework and harder feelings. Trust grows slowly through many small kept promises. Some friendships last for years without daily meetings. Others grow strong within one difficult week. Social media makes it easy to have hundreds of online friends. However, only a few people will call when you are truly in trouble. Choose friends who make you want to become better. A good friend is a quiet treasure.",
"Quarrels between close friends can feel like the end of the world. One careless word may hurt more than a physical wound. Pride often keeps both sides waiting for the other to speak. Wise friends know that sorry is a short word with great power. A sincere apology rebuilds a bridge that anger burnt down. Sometimes a quarrel makes a friendship stronger than before. The key is to talk about the problem, not about the person. Forgiveness frees the heart that carries it. A friendship that survives storms deserves deep trust.",
"Peer pressure is one of the strongest forces in teenage life. When friends laugh at a rule, it feels natural to break it too. Students may copy homework or buy expensive shoes to fit in. Psychologists say real friends never force you against your values. Saying no politely but firmly is a skill everyone can learn. A simple 'I am not interested' often ends the pressure. Joining groups with healthy habits protects students from trouble. Standing alone for a while beats falling with the crowd. Character is built in the moments you choose yourself.",
"Online friendship has become part of teenage social life. Students chat with players, classmates and strangers from other cities. Online friends understand hobbies that family members may not share. They can encourage each other through difficult exam weeks. However, the screen hides faces and sometimes true intentions. Students should never share home addresses or photos with strangers. Meeting an online friend alone is risky and unwise. Talking with parents about online friends keeps everyone safe. A real friendship grows in honesty, not in hiding. A true friend, near or far, wishes you well.",
# —— ⑭ 天气与自然 ——
"Typhoons are powerful storms that visit South China every summer. They form over warm seas and grow as they move towards land. Heavy rain, strong winds and huge waves arrive together. Before a typhoon, people bring in laundry and stock food and water. Schools may close, and flights are often cancelled. In 2018, a strong typhoon hit Guangdong and broke many trees. Students learned that nature's power deserves deep respect. After the storm, neighbours helped clear the streets together. Respect the sky, and the sky will spare you.",
"Rainbows appear when sunshine meets falling rain. Light enters each water drop and bends into seven colours. Red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo and violet form a bright arch. Ancient people told many stories about the colourful bridge. Scientists now explain the beauty with simple physics. A rainbow usually lasts only a few minutes. Photographers chase them across fields and city roofs. Seeing one after a storm feels like a gift from the sky. Children run outside and point at the magic in the air. Beauty often follows the hardest weather.",
"Fog is common in river cities during early spring mornings. Tiny water drops hang in the air and hide far buildings. Streets sound softer because fog swallows noise like a blanket. Drivers must slow down and turn on their lights. Students walk to school through a grey, quiet world. Fog usually melts away when the sun rises higher. Old people say fog days are good for the skin and the crops. In some cities, fog becomes a famous reason to visit. Walking in fog teaches patience in a hurry-up world.",
"The four seasons paint the year in different colours. Spring brings soft rain and green buds on every branch. Summer covers the fields with gold and the air with heat. Autumn turns leaves red and yellow before they fall. Winter wraps the ground in white and silences the streets. Each season has its own work: planting, growing, harvesting and resting. Farmers follow this rhythm more carefully than any calendar. City students can feel it in the food on their tables. Living with the seasons is living with wisdom.",
# —— ⑮ 安全与自护 ——
"Safety rules look boring until the day they save a life. In a fire, smoke rises and clean air stays near the floor. Students should crawl low and cover their mouths with wet cloth. Lifts must never be used during a fire, only the stairs. Schools practise fire drills twice a term for this reason. On the road, the green light still means look both ways. Bicycles need bells and lights for riding after sunset. Swimming alone in rivers is the most dangerous summer habit. A careful student checks the risk before the fun.",
"Earthquake drills teach students to react within seconds. When the ground shakes, the safest place is beside a strong desk. Students should protect their heads and hold the desk legs. Running to the stairs during the shaking is extremely dangerous. After the shaking stops, everyone walks to the open playground. Schools check that every student is safe and calm. Families should prepare a bag with water, food and a torch. Knowing the meeting place matters more than running fast. Prepared people survive, and panicked people suffer.",
"First aid skills should belong to everyone, not only doctors. If a classmate falls and bleeds, press a clean cloth on the wound. Raising the injured part above the heart slows the bleeding. For a nosebleed, the head should lean slightly forward. Putting the head back is a common but harmful mistake. Students should call teachers or dial 120 in serious cases. Learning simple first aid takes only a few lessons. Our school invited a nurse to teach us last month. A calm helper is often more useful than a fast runner. Knowledge turns bystanders into protectors.",
"Online safety is as important as road safety. Students meet strangers, offers and links that look attractive. A strange link may steal passwords in one careless click. Strange messages are deleted, not answered. Passwords should be long and different for each account. Sharing class photos with location open invites real danger. If something online makes you uncomfortable, tell a parent at once. Our school held a talk on internet safety last week. Experts showed how thieves trick young users with free gifts. Protect your information as you protect your home.",
# —— ⑯ 饮食与习惯 ——
"Breakfast is the meal that students skip most often. Some wake up late and rush out with only a bottle of water. An empty stomach cannot support four hours of morning lessons. Studies show that breakfast eaters remember words more easily. A good breakfast mixes grain, protein and some fruit. Bread, an egg and a glass of milk take ten minutes to prepare. Students who eat breakfast feel less hungry at lunch. Therefore they avoid unhealthy snacks between classes. A morning meal is a small gift to your future self.",
"Street food smells wonderful but needs careful choices. Vendors sell grilled corn, cakes and tea at every school gate. Some stalls are clean, while others use old oil and dirty water. Students should look for stalls with long queues and fresh food. Eating too much fried food causes spots and stomach aches. Carrying your own water bottle saves money and reduces plastic. Washing hands before eating prevents many illnesses. A healthy snack can be fruit, nuts or yogurt. The stomach keeps a record of every careless bite.",
"Chinese food culture values balance as much as taste. A family meal usually mixes meat, vegetables and soup. Colours on the plate matter, because variety brings variety of nutrients. Southern cooks use a little sugar to bring out freshness. Northern cooking often uses more salt and stronger fire. Eating together slowly helps digestion and conversation. Doctors warn against eating while staring at screens. A balanced plate is a small picture of a balanced life. Sharing dishes around a table builds closeness like nothing else. The table is where families learn to talk again.",
"Food waste is a serious problem in school canteens. Surveys show that students leave about one fifth of their food. Some portions are simply too large for smaller students. Others take dishes they do not like and abandon them. Waste costs families money and harms the environment at once. Growing the wasted rice would need thousands of fields. Our canteen started a 'clean plate' campaign last month. Students who finish their meals get small fruit stickers. Respect for food begins with an honest look at your plate.",
# —— ⑰ 音乐与艺术 ——
"Music changes mood faster than any words. A sad song can hold a person gently on a bad day. Fast rhythms make cleaning and running feel like a game. Students who learn an instrument train patience and memory. Piano practice teaches the hands to do two jobs at once. Singing in a choir builds confidence in shy children. Music therapy now helps patients in many hospitals. In our school, the guitar club performs every Friday afternoon. Listen closely, and the heart hears more than the ear.",
"Street art has turned grey walls into open galleries. Some cities invite artists to paint walls near schools and stations. Colourful murals make waiting for a bus less boring. Good street art tells stories about the neighbourhood. However, painting on private walls without permission is still against the law. One famous artist leaves small figures in cities around the world. Students in our class designed a wall about protecting the river. The whole school voted for the best painting. Beauty in public places belongs to no one and everyone.",
"Museums are not boring halls for old objects. A good museum tells stories, and objects are only the characters. The Guangdong Museum shows silk, ships and Cantonese opera masks. Visitors can even make a small clay pot in the workshop. Modern museums use lights, music and screens to wake up history. Many museums now offer free entry to students. Last month, our class sketched ancient vases for two hours. A girl who hated history called the trip surprisingly cool. Museums keep the past alive for the curious.",
"Photography helps students notice what they usually ignore. A phone camera is enough to start this hobby. Beginners should watch light first, because morning light is soft. Simple subjects, such as a grandmother's hands, tell deep stories. Taking too many photos stops the eye from really seeing. Editing can improve a picture, but it cannot add feeling. Our school photo club held a small exhibition last term. One photo of a rainy playground won the first prize. Every street holds a picture nobody has taken yet.",
# —— ⑱ 考试与心态 ——
"Exam anxiety is common, and it can be managed. Before a test, the body prepares as if facing danger. A fast heartbeat and sweaty hands are normal signals. Students can calm down with slow breathing for one minute. Good sleep the night before matters more than late revision. During the exam, easy questions come first to build confidence. Difficult questions are marked and returned to later. A clear plan prevents panic when time runs short. A steady mind is the best pen a student can hold.",
"Grades matter, but they are not the whole story. A mark shows how much you remembered on one day. It cannot measure courage, kindness or imagination. Students with low marks often grow into creative adults. History remembers people who kept learning after school ended. Schools now record effort and progress as well as scores. The purpose of exams is to find what to improve next. Effort recorded today becomes confidence tomorrow. A bad mark is information, not a definition of the self. Use grades as mirrors, not as cages.",
"Mistakes in exams are valuable gifts in disguise. Every wrong answer marks a hole in understanding. Students who copy answers lose this precious information. A mistake notebook is the tool of top learners. Write the question, the wrong answer and the correct reason. Review the notebook before every test, not only after one. Slowly, the same mistakes stop appearing in papers. Fear of mistakes kills learning faster than mistakes themselves. A corrected error is a lesson written in your own hand. The student who owns a mistake never repeats it.",
"Some students shine in exams but freeze on stage. Others struggle with tests yet speak with shining confidence. Schools now provide stages, sports fields and workshops for everyone. Different talents grow in different soils. Comparing yourself with others wastes the energy you need. The only fair comparison is with your own past self. Set a small goal this week and check it honestly. Different flowers open at different times in the same garden. Progress feels slow until one day it becomes visible. The quiet seed often becomes the tallest tree.",
# —— ⑲ 梦想与职业 ——
"Choosing a future job starts with knowing yourself. Some students love numbers and quiet analysis; others need people and noise. Career tests can help, but real experience teaches more. Try helping in a shop, a library or a clinic for one week. Talk with adults about the difficult parts of their work, not only the glory. New jobs appear every year that schools do not teach yet. Drone pilots, data helpers and game designers are real careers. Whatever the job, curiosity and communication always pay.",
"Being a doctor means carrying heavy responsibility. Medical students study for more than ten years before working alone. They meet fear, pain and sometimes death every week. However, they also see miracles that ordinary people never see. A good doctor treats the person, not only the illness. Nurses work equally hard and stay closest to patients. Our class interviewed a doctor from the city hospital last month. She said the hardest part is telling bad news gently. Medicine is a science of the head and an art of the heart.",
"Teachers shape the future in ways that rarely make headlines. A teacher may change a student's life with one sentence of belief. The job demands patience because progress is often invisible. Lesson preparation continues long after students go home. Modern teachers also learn to use videos and online tools. They guide students through stress as much as through grammar. In many countries, teachers receive too little respect. Yet no hospital, bridge or company works without them. A teacher's influence quietly outlives every lesson. The chalk dust settles, but the lesson stays.",
"Farmers feed cities, but few teenagers want to be one. Farming means early mornings, dirty hands and dependence on weather. Modern farms now use drones, sensors and online sales. Young farmers with phones sell rice directly to city families. Some return home from universities to run family farms. They earn well and live close to the soil. Agriculture is becoming a high-tech profession. Choosing farming is choosing a slow and honest life. Modern farming needs both strong backs and clever minds. The next meal on your table began in someone's field.",
# —— ⑳ 社区与邻里 ——
"A good neighbourhood is a small community with a big heart. Residents greet each other and watch over empty flats. Somebody always notices a parcel left at the wrong door. Community boards share news about repairs and lost cats. Volunteer groups organise cleaning days and free classes. Elderly residents teach children calligraphy in the small yard. During the pandemic, neighbours bought medicine for each other. Trust in a neighbourhood grows through hundreds of small favours. Small hellos turn strangers into familiar faces. The nearest help often lives one floor away.",
"Community libraries bring reading to people who cannot travel. Many cities place small bookshops in old bus stations and parks. Anyone can borrow a book with a simple card or a phone scan. Volunteers manage the shelves and keep the books clean. Children in poor areas find picture books after school. Some libraries also hold free talks on weekends. A small library can change the rhythm of a whole street. Reading spaces show that a city values its people. Knowledge shared in public stays with the public.",
"Old communities gain new life through careful repair. Instead of pulling down old buildings, cities now add lifts and gardens. Wide doors and gentle slopes help elderly residents. Small parks replace unused parking corners. Coloured walls tell the history of the street in paintings. Residents vote on every change before work begins. Our neighbourhood added a reading room last spring. Grandparents play chess there while children draw beside them. A lift can carry more than people; it carries dignity. Renewal is not only about new stones.",
"Community sports events bring different ages together. Every May, our street holds a fun sports day for all residents. Grandfathers race in slow bicycle contests. Mothers and daughters win the three-legged race. Retired teachers referee the children's football match. Winners receive plants instead of plastic medals. Even the losers went home with a small green plant. The day ends with a shared dinner of dumplings. People who never spoke before become team partners. Children learn that winning matters less than playing together. Laughter is the real score of the day.",
# —— ㉑ 发明与科学 ——
"Great inventions often begin with a small annoyance. The inventor of sticky notes simply wanted a bookmark that stayed. A Frenchman wrapped potatoes in foil and discovered the chip. Penicillin appeared when a scientist noticed mould on a plate. Curiosity turns small observations into world-changing tools. Students can train this habit by asking why every day. Keep a notebook of strange questions and possible answers. School science fairs are the first step on this road. The next big idea may sit in your pocket notebook.",
"Paper changed how human knowledge travelled. Before paper, people wrote on heavy bamboo and costly silk. Cai Lun improved paper-making in China around 105 AD. The technique slowly spread along trade roads to the West. Cheap paper made books available to ordinary people. Schools, libraries and science grew faster than ever before. Today, paper faces competition from screens. However, paper remains cheap, reliable and easy on the eyes. Writing turns a thought into a gift for the future. Every sheet carries two thousand years of history.",
"The compass opened the age of great sea voyages. Chinese sailors used it centuries before European explorers. A tiny needle always points to the magnetic north. With it, ships could cross oceans instead of hugging coasts. Trade, culture and knowledge flowed between continents. However, the compass could not prevent storms or hunger. Technology helps, but courage completes the journey. Modern phones still contain a compass inside them. Small tools can turn the whole world into a neighbourhood. Knowing where you are is the first step to anywhere.",
"Science fairs turn school corridors into laboratories. Students bring vinegar volcanoes, potato batteries and model rockets. Judges ask not only what happened but why it happened. One student tested which bag keeps ice longest. Another measured how music affects plant growth. Fair projects teach students to record data honestly. Failed experiments still earn points when the report is clear. Last year, a quiet girl won with a simple water filter. A curious question is the smallest experiment of all. Science begins when a student decides to test instead of guess.",
# —— ㉒ 诚实与品格 ——
"Honesty is tested in small, ordinary moments. Nobody watches you find a wallet full of money on the pavement. The owner's photo inside makes the choice suddenly real. Returning it may cost you time, but it builds your name. A reputation for honesty opens doors that money cannot. Students sometimes copy homework and call it helping. Such help steals learning from the helper too. Cheating in exams cheats the cheater most of all. Truth has a quiet weight that lies can never carry. A clear conscience sleeps better than a clever lie.",
"Responsibility shows itself in what you do unasked. A responsible student packs the bag the night before. They check the timetable instead of asking classmates. At home, they empty the dishwasher without being told. Responsibility is not a heavy duty; it is a quiet habit. People who own small tasks are trusted with big ones. Our class chose monitors for one week each. The shyest student surprised everyone by doing the job perfectly. Small duties completed build a strong character. Respect follows those who keep their own house in order.",
"Gratitude turns ordinary days into rich ones. People who count their blessings sleep better and smile more. A simple thank-you costs nothing and warms the whole room. Students can write three good things before sleeping each night. The list may include a friend's joke or a warm bowl of soup. Comparing with others steals gratitude; comparing with your past grows it. Difficult days teach lessons that easy days never offer. My grandmother thanks even the rain for watering her plants. Say thanks aloud, and the world answers kindly.",
"Perseverance separates dreams from wishes. Everyone wants to speak English fluently until the first hundred words. Everyone wants to run until the first aching kilometre. The difference appears in the boring middle of the road. Champions simply continue when excitement fades away. Small daily practice beats occasional heroic effort. Thomas Edison tested a thousand materials before the lamp worked. Persistence is talent with time added to it. He called each failure a step that did not work. The road itself becomes shorter for those who keep walking.",
# —— ㉓ 零花钱与消费 ——
"Pocket money teaches money lessons before real salaries arrive. Some parents give money weekly; others prefer monthly sums. Weekly money suits younger children who plan poorly. Monthly money trains older students to spread spending. Experts suggest letting children make small money mistakes early. A wasted toy teaches more than a long lecture. Students can keep a simple notebook of income and spending. Saving for a goal makes the purchase twice as sweet. Money wisely managed becomes freedom, not a chain. The first coin a child manages is a lesson for life.",
"Advertising surrounds teenagers with carefully designed messages. Colourful packets promise happiness in one bite. Famous faces suggest that buying a drink buys a lifestyle. Students should ask who paid for the message and why. Advertisements are legal, but their job is to sell, not to inform. Comparing prices per kilo reveals the real bargain. Reading the ingredient list beats believing the front picture. A delayed purchase often removes the desire completely. Every advert wants your attention; spend it wisely. Your pocket is the only vote that truly counts.",
"Sharing economy apps changed how young people travel. Students now rent bikes with a phone scan instead of buying one. Shared chargers appear in stations and cafes. Second-hand apps give old books and clothes a second life. This model saves money and reduces waste at once. However, shared items need care from every user. A damaged bike costs the next rider time and money. Digital manners matter as much as real ones. Respect turns sharing into a better deal for everyone. The commons survives on small courtesies.",
"Learning to budget is a survival skill for university life. Many freshmen spend their monthly money in the first week. Instant noodles appear at the end of every month. Simple budgets divide money into food, study and fun parts. Tracking spending for one month reveals surprising habits. A coffee a day can cost more than a library card. Students can cook simple meals and save a small fortune. Part-time work teaches the value of an hour. A notebook and a pen can protect a whole semester.",
# —— ㉔ 户外与露营 ——
"Camping brings families back to simple pleasures. A tent, two sleeping bags and a small stove are enough. The first task is choosing flat ground away from rivers. Building a fire needs dry wood and adult supervision. Dinner tastes better when cooked over a small flame. At night, the sky fills with stars that city lights hide. Children learn to identify the Big Dipper and listen for owls. Rain tests everyone's patience and waterproof bags. Sleeping under the stars resets a tired mind. Nature asks little and gives much.",
"Hiking is the cheapest ticket to a beautiful view. Good shoes matter more than expensive equipment. Walk slowly and breathe through the nose on steep paths. Rest every hour, even when the legs still feel strong. Carry water and a small snack, but leave no rubbish behind. Tell someone your route before entering mountain areas. In fog, stay on the marked path and never take shortcuts. Our class climbed a small mountain last autumn. The mountain does not move, so the walker must. The best views always cost effort.",
"Cycling to school is both exercise and freedom. Students feel the wind and arrive awake and warm. Bikes cost little and never wait in traffic jams. However, city roads demand full attention every second. Riders must stop at red lights like any car. Wearing a helmet protects the most important part of the body. Riding two people on one bike is dangerous and breaks traffic rules. A helmet is small, but it decides everything. Our city built safe bike lanes along the river last year. Pedals turn effort into distance and health.",
"Outdoor survival skills are returning to school camps. Students learn to build shelters from branches and leaves. They practise making fire without matches and finding clean water. A compass and a simple map beat a phone with no signal. Teachers say the skills build calm in difficult situations. One exercise sends teams to find their way back alone. Students who panic get lost; students who think clearly return. Nature rewards patience and punishes hurry. Knowing how to survive makes everyday worries smaller. The forest teaches what classrooms cannot.",
# —— ㉕ 野生动物与自然保护 ——
"The South China tiger may already be gone from the wild. Forty years ago, hunters and forest loss nearly finished the species. Scientists now keep the remaining tigers in breeding centres. Their dream is to return strong tigers to real mountains. Protecting a top predator protects the whole food chain. Without tigers, deer and rabbits would eat the forest bare. The story warns that extinction is forever. Every species lost closes a door that cannot reopen. One animal saved is one world kept. The wild needs witnesses, not only visitors.",
"Coral reefs are underwater cities full of colour. Small animals build stone homes over hundreds of years. Fish, crabs and octopuses hide among the branches. Reefs also protect coasts from strong waves. Warmer seas and dirty water are turning corals white. A white reef is a sick reef that may soon die. Divers in Hainan now plant young corals on metal frames. Students can help by using less plastic near the sea. A reef takes centuries to grow and days to destroy. Diving with eyes, never with hands, is the golden rule.",
"National parks protect land for both animals and people. China has established hundreds of them in recent years. Rangers walk long paths every day to watch for fires and hunters. Some parks allow visitors on wooden paths that protect plants. Cameras in the forest record pandas, bears and rare birds. The pictures help scientists count animals without disturbing them. Local people work as guides and sell farm products to tourists. Wild land is not empty; it is full of quiet work. A nation is rich when its rivers run clean.",
"Nature documentaries bring the wild into living rooms. Filmmakers wait weeks in hides for one minute of footage. Drones now film whales without disturbing their paths. These films teach millions about animals they will never meet. However, watching is not the same as protecting. Students can join local groups that clean rivers and count birds. Even a small balcony garden feeds city bees. Future generations deserve to hear real birdsong. Every species saved is a page kept in the book of life. Protect nature, and it will keep telling its story.",
]
